  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard to look at the panels, and I haven't seen the rule run.\n\n**Data:** The logs have a `timestamp` field and fields for the response code, bytes, file extension, source and destination geography, client IP, host, machine OS and agent. `response` is a string field, so I filter and group on `response.keyword`.\n\n**Dashboard** \"EFF-650313bb Web logs overview\" (id `eff-650313bb-web-logs-overview-a079c3529ac7`) has five panels over the last 7 days:\n- Total requests\n- Requests over time\n- Requests by response code\n- Average bytes by file extension\n- Top source countries\n\nThe dashboard is at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-650313bb-web-logs-overview-a079c3529ac7.\n\n**Rule** \"EFF-650313bb 5xx spike\" (id `77f2f203-d51f-4f2f-aa87-f2640fec3755`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It runs every minute and counts documents matching `response.keyword: 5*` in the last 5 minutes. It fires when that count is above 10. It has no action attached, so it will show up as an alert in Kibana but won't send a notification anywhere.",
